CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the four-step heterogeneous catalytic mechanism for the Ni-catalyzed hydrogenation of ethylene (C2H4 + H2 → C2H6) using ball-and-stick molecular models positioned above a green lattice representing the nickel surface. In panel (a), white spheres representing hydrogen atoms are shown adsorbed across the Ni surface after H–H bond cleavage, forming Ni–H bonds. Panel (b) shows an ethylene molecule (black carbon atoms double-bonded, white hydrogen atoms) descending onto the surface, with downward arrows and the label "Ethylene adsorbed on surface breaking π bonds" indicating that the C–C π-bond breaks and new Ni–C bonds form. Panel (c) depicts the surface-diffusion step, with curved arrows on the two carbon atoms showing H atoms migrating across the surface to collide with and bond to the adsorbed carbon atoms, forming new C–H bonds. Panel (d) shows the fully saturated ethane molecule (C2H6) departing upward from the now mostly bare Ni surface via a single upward arrow, representing desorption of the product. This figure establishes the mechanistic basis for heterogeneous catalysis by showing how the Ni surface facilitates bond breaking and formation at each discrete step of the hydrogenation reaction.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Green nickel surface slab drawn as a flat parallelogram plate with visible thickness (dark green side faces, thin yellow-green edge highlight line)
  - Regular close-packed grid of green hemispherical Ni atoms bumping up from the slab's top face (approx. 7 x 5 array)
  - Free H2 molecule(s) hovering above the slab: two white spheres joined by a short bond, tilted, with a downward arrow indicating adsorption (panel a)
  - Dissociated hydrogen atoms: individual white spheres resting in hollows on the Ni surface (panels a, b, c)
  - Ethylene molecule C2H4: two black carbon spheres joined by a double bond (two parallel bond rods), each carbon carrying two white hydrogens in a planar arrangement
  - Three downward arrows from the free ethylene to the surface (panel b)
  - Adsorbed ethylene on the surface: C=C converted to single C–C bond, carbons bonded down to the Ni surface, hydrogens bent upward (panel b, c)
  - Two curved arrows on the surface showing surface H atoms migrating toward each adsorbed carbon (panel c)
  - Ethane molecule C2H6: two black carbons single-bonded with three white hydrogens each, staggered, lifting off the surface (panel d)
  - Single upward curved arrow from the bare surface to the departing ethane (panel d)
  - Leader-line text labels: 'Ni surface', 'Hydrogen', 'Ethylene', 'Ethylene adsorbed on surface breaking π bonds'
  - Panel captions (a), (b), (c), (d) beneath the slab

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Single slab centered at origin: X (screen-right) 5.0 units wide, Y (screen-up) 0.35 units thick, Z (receding diagonal) 3.6 units deep; top face at y=0. Top face tiled with a 7 (X) x 5 (Z) array of green Ni hemispheres, radius 0.32, spacing 0.68 in X and 0.72 in Z, slightly offset rows for close packing; sphere green #4FA22E with lighter highlight #7CC243, slab side faces darker #2E7A1E, a thin yellow-green line #C8D64B along the top edge of the side faces (as in source). Atom sizes: carbon sphere r=0.33 dark charcoal #3A3A3A with grey specular; hydrogen sphere r=0.20 white #F2F2F2; bond rods r=0.055 light grey #CCCCCC. Molecular geometry: free ethylene planar, C–C = 0.90 units drawn as TWO parallel rods offset ±0.07 perpendicular to the axis (double bond), each C carrying 2 H at 120° in-plane, C–H = 0.62; molecule floats ~1.6 units above the slab, its plane roughly horizontal/tilted toward the viewer as in panel (b). Free H2: two white spheres, bond length 0.42, hovering 1.4 units above the surface at the right of the slab, tilted ~30° from horizontal. Adsorbed H atoms rest in hollows at y=+0.22, scattered 3–4 per panel (left-front and right-back of the slab, matching source). Adsorbed ethylene: C–C single rod, carbons at y=+0.55 directly over two adjacent hollows, each carbon's two H bent upward ~30° above horizontal; faint Ni–C bonds drawn as short dashed grey rods to the lattice. Ethane (panel d): staggered C2H6, C–C=0.90, three H per carbon at tetrahedral 109.5°, positioned 1.8 units above a now-nearly-bare slab (one stray white H left on the surface). Arrows: straight black arrows 0.9 units long pointing down in (a) and (b); two small black curved arrows (radius ~0.35) hugging the surface pointing inward toward each carbon in (c); one curved black arrow sweeping up-left from the slab to the ethane in (d). Labels in black sans-serif with thin black leader lines: 'Ni surface' and 'Hydrogen' upper-left (a), 'Ethylene' above (b), 'Ethylene adsorbed on surface breaking π bonds' below (b); panel caption letter centered below the slab. Background white.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "mechanismStep",
    "type": "state",
    "label": "Mechanistic stage",
    "states": [
      "(a) H2 adsorbs & dissociates",
      "(b) Ethylene adsorbs, π bond breaks",
      "(c) H atoms migrate & add",
      "(d) Ethane desorbs"
    ],
    "default": "(a) H2 adsorbs & dissociates",
    "teaches": "The hydrogenation proceeds as four discrete surface events, each with specific bonds broken (H–H, C=C π) and formed (Ni–H, Ni–C, C–H)"
  },
  {
    "id": "playCycle",
    "type": "animation",
    "label": "Play catalytic cycle",
    "loop": true,
    "teaches": "The steps are one continuous cycle: the Ni surface is regenerated bare after ethane leaves, ready to bind more reactants",
    "loopNote": "advances continuously through a→b→c→d"
  },
  {
    "id": "pickSurfaceAtom",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Every bump in the lattice is a Ni atom whose partially filled d-orbitals bond to adsorbates, weakening H–H and C=C bonds so they break at low temperature"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Adsorption is a three-dimensional event: H atoms sit in surface hollows and both carbons bind down to the plane, which only reads correctly when the slab is viewed from different angles"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "(a) Hydrogen adsorbs and dissociates",
    "narration": "An H2 molecule drops onto the nickel lattice; the H–H bond breaks and two separate H atoms sit bonded in surface hollows.",
    "state": {
      "mechanismStep": "(a) H2 adsorbs & dissociates"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "(b) Ethylene adsorbs, π bond breaks",
    "narration": "Ethylene descends onto the surface. Its C=C π bond opens, leaving a C–C single bond with each carbon now bonded to Ni and the hydrogens bent upward.",
    "state": {
      "mechanismStep": "(b) Ethylene adsorbs, π bond breaks"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "(c) Surface hydrogens migrate and add",
    "narration": "Adsorbed H atoms diffuse across the nickel and collide with the bound carbons; curved arrows show each new C–H bond forming as the Ni–C bonds weaken.",
    "state": {
      "mechanismStep": "(c) H atoms migrate & add"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "(d) Ethane desorbs",
    "narration": "With both carbons saturated, ethane no longer binds the metal and lifts off, leaving the nickel surface unchanged and available for the next cycle.",
    "state": {
      "mechanismStep": "(d) Ethane desorbs"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 45,
  "elevationDeg": 25,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.6
}

NOTES: Use MeshPhongMaterial/MeshStandardMaterial spheres with one key directional light plus ambient to reproduce the glossy ball-and-stick look. Render Ni lattice as InstancedMesh of hemispheres (SphereGeometry with phiLength cut, or full spheres sunk into a BoxGeometry slab) for performance; raycast the instanced mesh for the click interaction and pop an HTML tooltip 'Ni — surface atom' plus an info card, and tint the picked atom (emissive orange #FF8C3A) with a halo ring. Keep an OrthographicCamera for the axonometric feel. Transition between mechanism states by tweening atom positions (e.g. via a normalized 0→1 easing per step) so the animation and the state stepper share one timeline; the animation simply auto-advances the same parameter. Draw bond rods as CylinderGeometry oriented by quaternion between atom centers and rebuild/fade them when bonds break (fade the second π rod's opacity to 0 during step b) and form (grow C–H rods during step c). Arrows as ArrowHelper for straight arrows and TubeGeometry along a QuadraticBezierCurve3 with a cone tip for curved ones. Labels as CSS2DRenderer HTML elements with SVG/line leader lines so text stays screen-facing during orbit.

